msgid "Docker deployment"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This guide explains how to run CMS using Docker. No prior Docker "
"experience is required — just follow the steps below."
msgstr ""

msgid "What you need first"
msgstr ""

msgid "[Docker](https://docs.docker.com/get-docker/) (version 24 or newer)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"[Docker Compose](https://docs.docker.com/compose/install/) v2.24 or newer"
" (included with Docker Desktop; on Linux install the `docker-compose-"
"plugin` package)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A machine with Linux and cgroups v2 enabled (required by the sandbox). "
"Most modern Linux distros (Ubuntu 22.04+, Debian 12+, Fedora 36+) have "
"this enabled by default."
msgstr ""

msgid "Step 1 — Get the code"
msgstr ""

msgid "Step 2 — Create your configuration file"
msgstr ""

msgid "Copy the example file and open it in any text editor:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The file has comments explaining every option. At a minimum you **must** "
"fill in the values marked `CHANGE_ME`:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Variable"
msgstr ""

msgid "What it is"
msgstr ""

msgid "`CMS_SECRET_KEY`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A random 16-byte key used to protect cookies. Generate one with the "
"command shown in the file."
msgstr ""

msgid "`CMS_DB_URL`"
msgstr ""

msgid "The connection string to the PostgreSQL database."
msgstr ""

msgid "`POSTGRES_PASSWORD`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Password for the Docker-managed PostgreSQL database (required for Option "
"A in `.env.example`: PostgreSQL run by Docker, the `y` answer in Step 3)."
" Must match the password in `CMS_DB_URL`."
msgstr ""

msgid "`CMS_ADMIN_USER`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Username for the initial admin account created on first run. Can be "
"removed after the first deploy."
msgstr ""

msgid "`CMS_ADMIN_PASSWORD`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Password for the initial admin account created on first run. Can be "
"removed after the first deploy."
msgstr ""

msgid "`CMS_RWS_PASSWORD`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Password CMS uses to send scores to the Ranking Web Server. Its port is "
"published, so anyone who knows this password can change the public "
"scoreboard: never keep `CHANGE_ME`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Everything else has a sensible default and can be left as-is on the first"
" try."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`CMS_CONTEST_ID` is the numeric ID of the contest to serve, or `ALL` to "
"serve every active contest at once (see [Running several contests at once"
"](multi-contest.md)). It ships as `1`, the ID your first contest will "
"get: keep it on a fresh install. Do not remove it — without it no CMS "
"service starts, not even the Admin interface."
msgstr ""

msgid "Step 3 — Start CMS"
msgstr ""

msgid "The script asks two questions:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Use local PostgreSQL container?** — answer `y` if you want Docker to "
"manage PostgreSQL for you (recommended for a single server). Answer `n` "
"if you have an existing PostgreSQL server and already set `CMS_DB_URL` "
"accordingly. The answer is saved in `.env` as `CMS_USE_LOCALDB` and "
"becomes the default the next time a script asks."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Rebuild?** — a menu from 1 to 7. Choose `1) No` on the first run "
"(Docker builds the images that do not exist yet) or when nothing has "
"changed. After updating the code of a single-contest deployment, choose "
"`2) All services`, which rebuilds both images (`4) CMS only` leaves the "
"ranking on its old image). A multi-contest deployment follows the two "
"steps in [Running several contests at once](multi-contest.md) instead."
msgstr ""

msgid "Step 4 — Import a contest and set it as active"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Open the Admin interface in your browser at `http://your-server:8889` and"
" create your contest there. To restore a backup made with `./export.sh`, "
"use `./import.sh` instead: it reads the backup from the `dumps/` folder "
"(see [Docker Scripts Guide](docker-scripts.md))."
msgstr ""

msgid "Then run:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This lists all contests in the database, prompts you to pick one, updates"
" `CMS_CONTEST_ID` in `.env`, and optionally restarts services to apply "
"the change. It accepts only a numeric ID and replaces whatever was set "
"before, including `ALL`; to serve every active contest, set "
"`CMS_CONTEST_ID=ALL` in `.env` by hand."
msgstr ""

msgid "Ports"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"By default the following ports are exposed. You can change all of them in"
" `.env`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Port"
msgstr ""

msgid "Service"
msgstr ""

msgid "`8888`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Contest Web Server (contestants log in here)"
msgstr ""

msgid "`8889`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Admin Web Server (contest administration)"
msgstr ""

msgid "`8890`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Ranking Web Server (public scoreboard)"
msgstr ""

msgid "`9995`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"CMS-Loader (bulk user import; runs only when `CMS_LOADER_SESSION_SECRET`,"
" `CMS_LOADER_ADMIN_USER` and `CMS_LOADER_ADMIN_PASSWORD` are set, see "
"[CMS-Loader](cms-loader.md))"
msgstr ""

msgid "Common operations"
msgstr ""

msgid "**Follow live logs:**"
msgstr ""

msgid "**Stop everything:**"
msgstr ""

msgid "**Update to a newer version of this fork:**"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In `./up.sh`, choose a rebuild option as described in Step 3 (a multi-"
"contest deployment follows the two steps in [Running several contests at "
"once](multi-contest.md) instead). `./sync-upstream.sh` is not for this: "
"it is a tool for the fork's maintainers (see \"Helper scripts\" below)."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Run multiple Contest Web Server instances (for load balancing):**"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Set `CMS_CWS_COUNT=2` in your `.env`. CMS will start two contest web "
"servers on consecutive ports (8888 and 8889 with the default "
"`CMS_CWS_HTTP_PORT=8888`). The Admin and Ranking Web Server ports must "
"not fall in that range: set `CMS_AWS_HTTP_PORT` and `CMS_RWS_HTTP_PORT` "
"to `CMS_CWS_HTTP_PORT + CMS_CWS_COUNT` or higher (for example 8890 and "
"8891). Point your load balancer (e.g. nginx) at the contest web server "
"ports with `ip_hash`, as shown in \"Scaling Contest Web Server\" in the "
"[Docker Scripts Guide](docker-scripts.md)."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Running `docker compose` directly:**"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The next two commands call Docker Compose without a helper script, so "
"they must pass what the scripts add for you. Run them from the repo root "
"and:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"replace `cms-prod` after `-p` with your `CMS_PROJECT_NAME` (`cms-prod` is"
" the default). Without `-p`, Docker Compose works on a project named "
"`docker`, not on your deployment;"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"keep `--profile localdb` only if Docker manages your database (you "
"answered `y` to \"Use local PostgreSQL container?\"); remove it if you "
"use an external PostgreSQL server;"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"if you changed `CMS_CWS_HTTP_PORT`, put `CMS_CWS_HTTP_PORT_END=<last "
"Contest Web Server port>` in front of `docker compose` (that is "
"`CMS_CWS_HTTP_PORT + CMS_CWS_COUNT - 1`; the scripts compute it), or "
"Docker Compose may stop with `invalid containerPort`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Stop and delete all data (including a Docker-managed database — be "
"careful):**"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This deletes the Docker volumes of the deployment. With an external "
"PostgreSQL server it does not delete the database, which lives on that "
"server."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Re-run the database setup:**"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `db-init` service runs `cmsSetupDB`, which creates missing tables, "
"applies the fork's schema updates and creates the first admin if there is"
" none. It deletes nothing, so it is safe to re-run, and `./up.sh` already"
" runs it on every start. It does not reset the database: to start over "
"with an empty Docker-managed database, use the `down -v` command above, "
"then `./up.sh`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Run from a terminal, `cmsSetupDB` may ask questions. When the database "
"has no admin yet and `CMS_ADMIN_USER` and `CMS_ADMIN_PASSWORD` are not "
"set, it asks for an admin username and a password, and asks you to "
"confirm the password. When the database has no contests, it asks `No "
"contests found. Create a sample contest? [y/N]`: press Enter to skip it. "
"`./up.sh` does not ask these questions, because it starts `db-init` "
"detached, without a terminal."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Upgrading a deployment that was started before the `cms-data` volume "
"moved:**"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `cms-data` volume used to be mounted on `/home/cmsuser/cms/lib`, "
"which is also where the CMS code is installed. Docker filled the volume "
"from the first image and never refreshed it, so rebuilding the image did "
"not change the code the `cms` container ran. The volume is now mounted on"
" `/home/cmsuser/cms/data`, which holds only runtime data (submission and "
"user-test copies, Telegram bot state). It keeps its name and its "
"contents, so nothing is lost."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"After updating to this version you **must rebuild the CMS image**. For a "
"single-contest deployment run `./up.sh` and choose `4) CMS only` (or `2) "
"All services`). For a multi-contest deployment follow the two steps of "
"\"Deployment\" in [Running several contests at once](multi-contest.md): "
"`./up.sh` and `3) Ranking only` first (it starts only the ranking "
"container, so `cms` is not recreated from its old image), then `./up.sh` "
"and `4) CMS only`. Never start this version with an old image, which is "
"also what `1) No` does (the default in `./up.sh`, `./restart.sh` and "
"`./contest.sh`): the old image does not know the new `data_dir`, so "
"submission and user-test copies go to the container's own filesystem and "
"are lost the next time it is recreated, and the Telegram bot re-sends "
"every question and announcement. Once rebuilt, the `cms` container runs "
"the code of the image for real, possibly newer than what was running "
"until now."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The old copy of the code left in the volume, the `python3.12` directory, "
"is inert. To free the space, find the volume name with `docker volume ls "
"| grep cms-data` (with the default project name it is `cms-prod_cms-"
"data`) and delete that directory:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This removes only the stale code copy; `submissions/`, `tests/` and "
"`telegram/` in the volume are not touched. Do not roll back to a checkout"
" from before this change: its compose file mounts the volume over the "
"code again, so the `cms` container runs that stale copy instead of the "
"checkout's code, and if the directory was deleted it cannot start at all."
msgstr ""

msgid "Troubleshooting"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**A container exits or `./up.sh` times out:** Check the logs with "
"`./logs.sh`. The most common causes are:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`CMS_SECRET_KEY` is still set to the example value. The container keeps "
"running, but the Admin Web Server crashes with \"Non-hexadecimal digit "
"found\" and `./up.sh` gives up waiting after 90 seconds — generate a real"
" one with `openssl rand -hex 16`. The Contest Web Server fails the same "
"way once its contest exists; on a fresh install it stops earlier, on the "
"missing contest (see the next entry)."
msgstr ""

msgid "`CMS_DB_URL` is wrong or the database is unreachable."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"cgroups are not available on your machine — check that you are on a "
"modern Linux kernel (5.10+) with `cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cgroup.controllers`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**\"There is no contest with the specified id\" in the logs:** On a fresh"
" install `CMS_CONTEST_ID=1` points to a contest that does not exist yet, "
"so the Contest Web Server, ProxyService and EvaluationService stop with "
"this message, and so does the Telegram bot if you configured it "
"(`CMS_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN` and `CMS_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID`). This is expected: "
"the Admin interface still works. Create the contest there, set its ID "
"with `./contest.sh` (or set `CMS_CONTEST_ID=ALL` in `.env`) and restart "
"with `./restart.sh`. Do not remove `CMS_CONTEST_ID` to silence the "
"message: without it no CMS service starts."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Port already in use:** Change the corresponding `CMS_*_HTTP_PORT` "
"variable in `.env` and restart. If you run `docker compose` yourself, "
"always pass `--env-file .env` and the other options listed in \"Running "
"`docker compose` directly\" above — without `--env-file`, Docker Compose "
"cannot read the file because it lives in the repo root while the compose "
"file is in `docker/`, so the port variables fall back to their hardcoded "
"defaults (8888/8889/8890) regardless of what you set in `.env`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Helper scripts"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The repo root contains convenience wrappers around the `docker compose` "
"commands. Run them from the repo root — they read `.env` automatically."
msgstr ""

msgid "Script"
msgstr ""

msgid "What it does"
msgstr ""

msgid "`./up.sh`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Start services. Asks whether to use a local Docker-managed database "
"(`--profile localdb`) and whether to rebuild the image."
msgstr ""

msgid "`./down.sh`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Stop and remove all containers. Asks for confirmation first."
msgstr ""

msgid "`./restart.sh`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`down` followed by `up` (asks for confirmation, then prompts again for "
"local DB / rebuild)."
msgstr ""

msgid "`./logs.sh`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Show the last 100 log lines of all services; `./logs.sh -f` follows them "
"live."
msgstr ""

msgid "`./status.sh`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Show the running status of all containers."
msgstr ""

msgid "`./contest.sh`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Switch the active contest: lists available contests from the database, "
"prompts for a new ID, and updates `CMS_CONTEST_ID` in `.env`. Optionally "
"restarts services to apply the change."
msgstr ""

msgid "`./export.sh`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Back up contest data to a `.tar.gz` file in `dumps/`."
msgstr ""

msgid "`./import.sh`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Restore contest data from a backup in `dumps/` made with `./export.sh`."
msgstr ""

msgid "`./sync-upstream.sh`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For the fork's maintainers, not for updating a deployment: merges the "
"latest changes from the upstream `cms-dev/cms` repository into this "
"fork's `main` and pushes it to `origin`. Requires an `upstream` remote: "
"`git remote add upstream https://github.com/cms-dev/cms.git`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For more detail on each script see [Docker Scripts Guide](docker-"
"scripts.md)."
msgstr ""

